{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem visited, etc., as He had spoken.</b> That is: not because he prayed for Avimelekh and by virtue of the principle that whoever prays for his fellow, etc.; for without this she would not have been visited — and this would diminish, heaven forbid, the promise stated at the beginning of the portion. Rather, [the fulfillment came] כַּאֲשֶׁר אָמָר — as He had spoken." "<b>Furthermore,</b> always [the opposite is the case]: for his prayer on behalf of Avimelekh is the very thing that brought about Sarah's visitation. And His promise, may He be blessed, was that He would give him a son, so HaShem prepared for him a mitzvah whose special property (segullah) is that one is visited [with blessing] through it — and through this His promise was fulfilled. And this is what it says, <b>as He had spoken</b> — meaning: by virtue of the speaking (amirah) that HaShem spoke, it is that the matter came about and she was visited. For when HaShem desires to bestow good, He prepares a mitzvah whose special property is the sought-after good. And you will find that before the promise of the visitation, the matter of Pharaoh had already befallen Avraham, which is similar to the matter of Avimelekh, and HaShem did not restrain Pharaoh so that Avraham would pray for him — and this is for you a sign to what we have explained." "<b>And HaShem did, etc., as He had spoken.</b> The reason Scripture repeats by saying ״וַיַּעַשׂ״ etc., may be understood according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Tanhuma) on the verse ״כָּעֵת חַיָּה״, that the angel drew a line on the wall [indicating] when the sun reaches this place Sarah will have a son. And Scripture is informing us that the statement of the angel who spoke to her was fulfilled, with precise specification of the time." "<b>Furthermore,</b> Scripture may intend according to what I explained on the verse (Genesis 18:10) ״בֵּן לְשָׂרָה אִשְׁתֶּךָ״ — that [the child] came from the side of the feminine (sitra de-nukva), and for this reason the Akedah was necessary. And it is known that this is the cause of Sarah's death. And according to what I have written, that HaShem was exacting with Sarah's laughter and decreed that Sarah would not give birth except to a son from the side of the feminine (sitra de-nukva), as it says (Genesis 17:19) ״אֲבָל שָׂרָה אִשְׁתְּךָ יוֹלֶדֶת״ — therefore it says <b>and HaShem did for Sarah as He had spoken</b>, and it is known that the attribute of speech (dibbur) is harsh, meaning that she did not give birth to him whole in the wholeness required, and the consequences played out and Sarah died because of this. And along this line, the fulfillment of the promise regarding the line that the angel drew for her is alluded to in the verse that follows this one, where it is written ״לַמּוֹעֵד אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר אֹתוֹ״ etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:43.912115781Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}